WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Elena receives a new life policy, later misses a premium,

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.300
and eventually faces a claim question.

3
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.060
Those events do not start the same clock.

4
00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:16.780
A return or free-look period gives the owner an opportunity to

5
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:20.380
review and cancel under the applicable notice.

6
00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:25.700
A grace period permits late payment of a premium after the

7
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.940
first while coverage continues under its terms.

8
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.160
An incontestability provision limits a later challenge to the policy's

9
00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.480
validity after the required lifetime period.

10
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.960
Identify the event and the purpose before choosing a number.

11
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.840
A familiar thirty-day answer can be wrong for the question actually

12
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.870
asked. Maryland's general notice provision gives the policyholder

13
00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:57.830
ten days after delivery to surrender the covered policy for cancellation

14
00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:03.490
with written notice. The statute describes a proportional refund for

15
00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:07.970
the unexpired term, or a similar notice no less favorable to

16
00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:12.270
the policyholder. A particular policy can give more favorable rights.

17
00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:17.490
A covered replacement instead carries the thirty-day return right taught in

18
00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:22.410
Part fifteen, with its ordinary full-refund rule and different

19
00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:27.670
variable-contract formula. Do not turn every free-look

20
00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:31.190
question into one universal refund promise.

21
00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:35.310
Read the transaction, delivered notice and applicable rule together.

22
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.820
Marcus chooses monthly payments for a policy with continuing required

23
00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:44.160
premiums. Paying the first installment does not make the contract paid

24
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.240
up. The payment mode describes the schedule, and the total annual

25
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.660
cost can differ from paying once per year.

26
00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:56.240
For example, twelve monthly payments of fifty-five dollars total six hundred

27
00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.740
sixty dollars, compared with an assumed six hundred dollar annual payment.

28
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:04.450
These are teaching amounts, not a carrier quote.

29
00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:09.080
Flexible-premium policies have different funding mechanics, but continuing

30
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:14.090
deductions still matter. Read due dates, accepted payment arrangements

31
00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:16.800
and the conditions needed to keep protection in force.

32
00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:22.050
Maryland's standard individual-life grace provision applies to premiums after

33
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:26.370
the first. It allows thirty days, or at the insurer's option

34
00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:28.570
one month of at least thirty days.

35
00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:33.610
A specified industrial-life schedule has a different four-week rule.

36
00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:36.990
The policy continues in full force during grace.

37
00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:41.630
If a covered claim arises then, a due or overdue premium

38
00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:43.980
may be deducted from the proceeds.

39
00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:48.770
Assume a payable death benefit of one hundred thousand dollars and

40
00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:53.430
six hundred dollars of overdue premium, with no other adjustment.

41
00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:57.810
Subtracting that premium leaves ninety-nine thousand four hundred dollars.

42
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:03.070
A missed due date alone did not immediately erase the protection.

43
00:03:03.744 --> 00:03:08.700
Maryland's standard individual-life provision makes the policy incontestable,

44
00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.040
except for nonpayment of premiums, after it has been in force

45
00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.404
during the insured's lifetime for two years after issue.

46
00:03:17.344 --> 00:03:19.004
The lifetime requirement matters.

47
00:03:19.744 --> 00:03:24.244
Death eighteen months after issue does not satisfy it merely because

48
00:03:24.244 --> 00:03:25.964
a claim is submitted later.

49
00:03:26.664 --> 00:03:31.324
During the applicable contestable period, a misstatement can require review

50
00:03:31.324 --> 00:03:35.584
under Maryland's materiality and other statutory tests.

51
00:03:36.404 --> 00:03:41.124
After the specified period, distinguish an attempted validity challenge from

52
00:03:41.124 --> 00:03:43.884
a defense based on a genuine coverage restriction.

53
00:03:44.944 --> 00:03:49.984
Incontestability is a particular contractual protection, not a promise that

54
00:03:49.984 --> 00:03:51.524
every event must be covered.

55
00:03:52.146 --> 00:03:55.026
Elena's policy has been in force during her lifetime for three

56
00:03:55.026 --> 00:03:58.046
years. Assume no separate coverage exclusion applies.

57
00:03:58.866 --> 00:04:02.213
She dies during its valid premium grace period, with a five

58
00:04:02.213 --> 00:04:06.406
hundred dollar premium overdue and a stated one hundred thousand dollar

59
00:04:06.406 --> 00:04:10.866
benefit. The insurer cannot simply call the policy lapsed on the

60
00:04:10.866 --> 00:04:13.326
missed due date when grace still protects it.

61
00:04:13.726 --> 00:04:17.314
Apply the permitted overdue-premium deduction, producing ninety-nine thousand

62
00:04:17.314 --> 00:04:19.466
five hundred dollars in this example.

63
00:04:20.246 --> 00:04:23.706
A proposed challenge to the original policy's validity is a different

64
00:04:23.706 --> 00:04:25.686
issue governed by incontestability.

65
00:04:26.186 --> 00:04:30.886
Keep payment status, coverage scope and validity in separate steps.
